     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. 6,400 mg/day is twice the maximum labeled dose. Ibuprofen has a ceiling effect for pain and inflammation, so doses above 3,200 mg/day add no benefit. They do raise the risk of serious GI bleeding, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events. I can't find a standard adult indication for this dose, and I don't want to put it into the system where it could be given.\n\nHere is what I can do right now:\n- **Enter a maximum-dose order:** for example, ibuprofen 800 mg PO four times daily (3,200 mg/day), with a note about the indication. I'll enter it if you tell me to.\n- **Enter a lower dose:** 400\u2013800 mg PO every 6\u20138 hours.\n- **Help with an alternative:** if pain control is the problem, adding scheduled acetaminophen or another analgesic class may work better than pushing ibuprofen higher.\n\nIf you think this dose is clinically necessary, for example under a specialist protocol, it should go through your pharmacy and a direct prescriber override in the order-entry system. That route has pharmacist review, which is the right safeguard for an order like this. I can't do that from here.\n\nWould you like me to enter one of the options above?"
      }
